<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-num-analysis" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Analyzing Equations Numerically</title>
    <objectives> 
        <ul>
        
            <li><p>To understand that numerical algorithms such as <term>Euler's method</term> allow the approximation of solutions to the initial value problems and that there are more efficient algorithms than Euler's method such as those algorithms that use the <term>Runge-Kutta methods</term>.</p></li>

            <li><p>To understand that <term>Taylor's Theorem</term> is a very useful tool for studying differential equations.</p></li>

            <li><p>To understand that error analysis of the rate of convergence is very important for any numerical algorithm.</p></li>

        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            As we've already seen, we can't always find a solution to a 
            first-order initial value problem
            <md number="yes" xml:id="eq-eulers-init-value">
                y'=f(x,y), \qquad y(x_0)=y_0
            </md>
            analytically. It could be the case that we cannot integrate a 
            function in terms of elementary functions, for instance. (It's 
            not you, it's the integral!) However, we may still need a 
            reasonable approximation of the solution curve to make estimates. 
            We will explore some methods in this section on 
            numerically approximating solutions to these problems.
        </p>
        <p>
            Our initial value problem provides us with one point on the desired 
            solution curve, namely <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>. Our goal is to produce a 
            set of discrete points
            <md>
                (x_0, y_0), (x_1,y_1), (x_2, y_2), \dots, (x_N, y_N)
            </md>
            that can approximate points on the solution curve. For this purpose, 
            we will insist that the <m>x</m>-coordinates be equally spaced. That 
            is,
            <md>
                x_j = x_0 + j\Delta x, \quad j = 1, 2, \dots, N
            </md>
            and the corresponding <m>y_j</m> is an approximation of the solution 
            at <m>x_j</m>; that is, <m>y_j\approx y(x_j)</m>. Once this list of points is 
            obtained, it is usually plotted by connecting the points by 
            straight-line segments; or, if you are using a sophisticated 
            computer package, a curve fitting routine may be used to fit a 
            smooth curve through the points.
        </p>
        <p>
            The presence of a <m>\Delta x</m> term may put us in mind of our 
            previous work on defining definite integrals in terms of Riemann 
            sums. As with that situation, our approximations improve as the step 
            size <m>\Delta x</m> shrinks, but the calculations become difficult, 
            especially because we humans are prone to rounding off answers, leading 
            to error accumulating as we perform more calculations. Using computers 
            can fend this off for awhile, but even computers have a maximum word size and 
            run into this issue after several steps. Also, different methods have 
            different error estimates based on the size of <m>\Delta x</m>. 
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-eulers-method">
        <title>Euler's method</title>
        <p>
            We have already seen the usefulness of slope (or direction) 
            fields in visualizing solution curves. If we consider the initial 
            value problem <xref ref="eq-eulers-init-value" />, we recall 
            that we would draw a short line segment of slope <m>m_0 = f(x_0, y_0)</m> 
            through the point <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>. This line segment is part of 
            the tangent line to the solution curve at <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <term>Euler's method</term> is one of the oldest and simplest 
            numerical methods for obtaining approximate solutions to the 
            initial value problem <xref ref="eq-eulers-init-value" />. 
            Visually, the method says that we would start at our initial 
            point <m>(x_0, y_0)</m> and follow the tangent line of slope 
            <m>f(x_0, y_0)</m> until we arrive at a point 
            <m>\Delta x</m> units to the right. This will bring us to the point 
            <m>(x_1,y_1)</m>. We recompute our slope as <m>f(x_1,y_1)</m> and 
            follow a line segment of this slope from <m>(x_1,y_1)</m> until we 
            arrive at a point <m>\Delta x</m> units further to the right. This 
            will bring us to the point <m>(x_2,y_2)</m>. We then adjust our slope 
            to be <m>f(x_2,y_2)</m> and follow the line segment of that slope from 
            <m>(x_2,y_2)</m> to <m>(x_3,y_3)</m>. We continue in this manner until 
            we reach the point <m>(x_N, y_N)</m>. (See <xref ref="fig-eulers-method" />.)
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-eulers-method">
            <caption>Euler's method</caption>
            <image xml:id="img-euler-method" width="80%">
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \begin{axis}[
                        axis x line=middle,
                        xmin=-1.5, xmax=5.5,
                        ymin=-0.2, ymax=5.5,
                        xtick={0, 1, 2, 3, 4},
                        xticklabels={$x_0$, $x_1$, $x_2$, $x_3$, $x_4$},
                        ytick={0},
                        xlabel={$x$},
                        ylabel={$y$},
                        samples=100
                    ]

                    % True solution curve 
                    \addplot[thick, blue, domain=0:4] {exp(0.4*x) + 0.5};

                    % Euler approximation segments
                    \addplot[dashed, red, thick, domain=0:1] {1.5 + 0.4*(x-0)};
                    \addplot[dashed, red, thick, domain=1:2] {1.9 + 0.5967*(x-1)};
                    \addplot[dashed, red, thick, domain=2:3] {2.4967 + 0.8902*(x-2)};
                    \addplot[dashed, red, thick, domain=3:4] {1.328*(x-3) + 3.3869}; 

                    % Points on the polygon approximation path
                    \fill[black] (axis cs:0,1.5) circle (2pt) node[left] {$(x_0, y_0)$};
                    \fill[black] (axis cs:1,1.9) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$(x_1, y_1)$};
                    \fill[black] (axis cs:2,2.49) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$(x_2, y_2)$};
                    \fill[black] (axis cs:3,3.39) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$(x_3, y_3)$};
                    \fill[black] (axis cs:4,4.7) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$(x_4, y_4)$};

                    % Delta annotations
                    \draw[dotted] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:0,1.5);
                    \draw[dotted] (axis cs:1,0) -- (axis cs:1,1.9);
                    \draw[dotted] (axis cs:2,0) -- (axis cs:2,2.49);
                    \draw[dotted] (axis cs:3,0) -- (axis cs:3,3.39);
                    \draw[dotted] (axis cs:4,0) -- (axis cs:4,4.7);

                    % Delta x arrows
                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (axis cs:0,0.5) -- (axis cs:1,0.5) node[midway, above] {$\Delta x$};
                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (axis cs:1,0.5) -- (axis cs:2,0.5) node[midway, above] {$\Delta x$};
                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (axis cs:2,0.5) -- (axis cs:3,0.5) node[midway, above] {$\Delta x$};
                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (axis cs:3,0.5) -- (axis cs:4,0.5) node[midway, above] {$\Delta x$};
 
                    \end{axis}
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
            Notice that no <em>derivatives</em> of <m>f</m> have to be evaluated, 
            just <m>f</m> itself.
        </p>
        <note>
            <title>Algorithm for Euler's Method</title>
            <p>Given <m>y'=f(x,y)</m>, <m>y(x_0)=y_0</m>, our goal is to find 
            approximate values of <m>y(x)</m> on the interval <m>x_0\le x\le 
            x_{\text{max}}</m>.</p>
            <p><ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Choose a small step size <m>h=\Delta x = \frac{x_{\text{max}}-x_0}{N}</m>, 
                        with <m>N</m> a positive integer.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        For <m>n=0, 1, 2, \dots, N-1</m>, compute
                        <md>
                            <mrow>x_{n+1} \amp = x_n + h </mrow>
                            <mrow>y_{n+1} \amp = y_n + f(x_n,y_n)\,h.</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Plot the points <m>(x_0, y_0), (x_1, y_1), \dots 
                        (x_N, y_N)</m> and connect them by drawing straight 
                        lines. This results in a piecewise linear approximation 
                        to <m>y(x)</m> on the interval <m>[x_0, x_\text{max}]</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol></p>
        </note>
        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Approximate the solution of <m>y'=x-y</m>, <m>y(0)=1</m> 
                    on the interval <m>[0,2]</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>We first note that <m>f(x,y)=x-y</m>.</p> 
                <p>
                    Let's first try this with <m>N=4</m> points. Then we have 
                    that <m>h = (2-0)/4 = 0.5</m>. It is helpful to make a 
                    table. The values <m>x_n = x_0 + n\cdot h</m> are all 
                    determined once <m>h</m> is chosen.
                </p>
                <table>
                    <title>Euler's method table</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="major">
                            <cell><m>n</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>x_n</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>y_n</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>f(x_n,y_n) = x_n-y_n</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>y_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n,y_n)\cdot h</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1+(-1)(0.5) = 0.5</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.5+(0)(0.5) = 0.5</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.5</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.5+(0.5)(0.5) = 0.75</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.75</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.75</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.75+(0.75)(0.5) = 1.125</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.125</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                Thus <m>\boxed{y(2)\approx 1.125}</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A couple of comments:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The value of <m>y_{n+1}</m> at the end of 
                                each row is used as the value of <m>y_n</m> 
                                in the following row. The values of 
                                <m>x_0</m> and <m>y_0</m> come from the 
                                initial condition we're given.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The function <m>f(x_n,y_n)</m> is the slope 
                                function and depends on the differential 
                                equation we're given.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We can use MATLAB to produce a table:
                            </p>
                            <program language="octave">
                                % Define the differential equation y' = f(x, y)
                                f = @(x, y) x - y;

                                % Initial conditions
                                x0 = 0;
                                y0 = 1;
                                xmax = 2;

                                % Configuration
                                num_steps = 4;      % Number of steps to generate 4 points after the initial condition
                                h = (xmax - x0)/num_steps;

                                % Initialize arrays to hold the values
                                x = zeros(num_steps + 1, 1);
                                y = zeros(num_steps + 1, 1);
                                f_val = zeros(num_steps + 1, 1);

                                % Set the initial point
                                x(1) = x0;
                                y(1) = y0;
                                f_val(1) = f(x(1), y(1));

                                % Euler's Method loop
                                for n = 1:num_steps
                                    % Calculate next y value: y_next = y_curr + h * f(x_curr, y_curr)
                                    y(n+1) = y(n) + h * f_val(n);

                                    % Advance x
                                    x(n+1) = x(n) + h;

                                    % Calculate f(x, y) at the new point (except for the last point, where it's optional)
                                    if n &lt; num_steps + 1
                                        f_val(n+1) = f(x(n+1), y(n+1));
                                    end
                                end

                                % Create and display the table
                                Step = (0:num_steps)';
                                EulerTable = table(Step, x, y, f_val, 'VariableNames', {'n', 'x_n', 'y_n', 'f(x_n,y_n)'});
                                disp(EulerTable);

                            </program>
                            <image source="./images/eulertable.png" width="60%"/>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The differential equation in this example is linear: 
                    <m>y'=x+y\Longrightarrow y'-y=x</m>. Using the method from 
                    <xref ref="sec-linear-eq" />, we get that <m>y=x-1+2e^{-x}</m>. 
                    (Check this!) This gives the exact value of
                    <md>
                        y(2) = 2-1+2e^{-2} \approx 1.27067.
                    </md>
                    The absolute error in our numerically computed value of 
                    <m>y(2)</m> in the above table is
                    <md>
                        |1.27067 - 1.125| \approx 0.14567.
                    </md>
                    If the calculations are redone with <m>N=8</m>, that is 
                    with <m>\Delta x = 0.25</m>, the corresponding table is
                </p>
                <table>
                    <title>Euler's method table</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="major">
                            <cell><m>n</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>x_n</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>y_n</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>f(x_n,y_n) = x_n-y_n</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>y_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n,y_n)\cdot h</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.75</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.25</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.75</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>-0.50</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.625</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.50</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.625</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>-0.125</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.59375</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.75</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.59375</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.15625</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.6328125</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.00</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.6328125</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.3671875</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.72460938</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>5</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.25</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.72460938</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.52539063</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.85595703</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>6</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.50</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.85595703</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.64404297</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.01696777</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>7</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.75</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.01696777</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0.73303223</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.20022583</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>8</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>2.00</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>1.20022583</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    Thus <m>\boxed{y(2)\approx 1.200226}</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The error in the approximate value of <m>y(2)</m> is now
                    <md>
                        |1.27067 - 1.200226|\approx 0.0704.
                    </md>
                    By cutting the step size <m>\Delta x</m> in half the 
                    error has been cut approximately in half, from 0.146 to 
                    0.070. <xref ref="fig-euler-graph" /> shows the two 
                    approximate solutions together plotted with the exact 
                    solution.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-euler-graph">
                    <caption>Euler's method approximations to <m>y'=x-y</m> 
                    with <m>N=4, 8</m></caption>
                    <image source="./images/eulergraph.png" width="80%" />
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use MATLAB to generate a table for the 
                    <m>N=8</m> case from the example.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
        <worksheet xml:id="ws-eulers-method">
            <title>In-Class Work on Euler's Method</title>
            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <p>
                    Use Euler's method to find an approximate value for 
                    <m>y(0.2)</m> to four decimal places, given that <m>y(x)</m> 
                    is the solution to the IVP
                    <md>
                        y' = x + y^2, \quad y(0) = 0
                    </md>
                    using first <m>h=0.1</m>, then <m>h=0.05</m>. Feel free 
                    to use your calculator or MATLAB to do the calculations.
                </p>
            </exercise>
            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <p>
                    Unlike the previous problem, the initial value problem 
                    <md>
                        y'=2xy, \quad y(1) = 1
                    </md>
                    can be solved by us by hand. (The equation is separable.)
                </p>
                <p>
                    Solve the equation by hand and determine the values of 
                    <m>y(1.1)</m>, <m>y(1.2), \dots</m> up to <m>y(1.5)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>Then</em> use Euler's method with <m>h=0.1</m> and 
                    compare the values you get for <m>y(1.1)</m>, <m>y(1.2), \dots</m> up to <m>y(1.5)</m>. 
                    Feel free to use your calculator or MATLAB to do the 
                    calculations.
                </p>
            </exercise>
        </worksheet>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-error-Euler">
        <title>Error and the Order of a Numerical Method</title>
        <p>
            We demonstrated Euler's method above on a differential equation we 
            knew how to solve; we would never use this method on such an equation. 
            (Why approximate when we can get an exact answer, and then express that 
            as a decimal?) This was done to convince you that such a method can 
            get results.
        </p>
        <p>
            We would use a numerical method in the case where we <em>can't</em> 
            get a solution analytically. Because of this, we are duty bound to 
            estimate the error in using the numerical method.
        </p>
        <p>
            There are two different types of error that can occur when solving a 
            differential equation numerically. The first is called <term>round-off 
            error</term> and is due to rounding off or chopping the result after 
            each step of the procedure, in order to store it on a computer with 
            fixed word size. For example, if <m>y(0) = \frac13</m>, and the computer 
            has a word size of 4 decimal digits, then it would probably store 
            <m>y(0)</m> as 0.3333, already introducing an error greater than 
            0.00003 into the calculation. Fortunately, modern computers can 
            store a lot more digits than this so it is usually not a problem, 
            unless the procedure requires a huge number of steps.
        </p>
        <p>
            The type of error you really need to be concerned about is 
            caused by the approximations made by whatever method you are 
            using. This is called <term>truncation error</term>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose we're trying to solve the initial value problem <m>y'=f(x,y)</m>, <m>y(x_0) = y_0</m> for 
            <m>y</m>. If the slope function <m>f</m> can be differentiated 
            enough times, <m>y(x)</m> could be approximated by the first <m>N+1</m> 
            terms of its Taylor series about <m>x=x_0</m>:
            <md number="yes" xml:id="eq-Taylor-series">
                y(x_0+h) = y(x_0) + y'(x_0)h + \frac{y''(x_0)h^2}{2!} + \cdots + 
                \frac{y^{(N)}(x_0)h^N}{N!} + E_N(h)
            </md>
            where the error <m>E_N(h)</m> is known by <term>Taylor's theorem</term> to be
            <md number="yes" xml:id="eq-Taylor-error">
                E_N(h) = \frac{x^{(N+1)}(\xi) h^{N+1}}{(N+1)!}
            </md>
            for <m>\xi</m> some value of <m>x</m> between <m>x_0</m> and 
            <m>x_0+h</m>.
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>